193	<body>
194	<div id="viewport"><div id="deck">
195	
196	<!-- ====================================================================== 01 -->
197	<section class="slide cover" data-title="Cover">
198	  <div class="rule top"></div><div class="rule bot"></div>
199	  <img class="mark-lg" src="assets/logo/smwu-logo-mark-mono.png" alt="">
200	  <div class="title-box">
201	    <h1>Simulating a Digital Twin:<br>Spring–Mass Model and Frictional Contact</h1>
202	  </div>
203	  <div class="meta"><div>Department of Mechanical Engineering</div><div>Advanced Deep Learning 2026</div><div>Joo Yong Sim</div></div>
204	  <div class="sub">PhysTwin and real2sim-eval read line by line — with measurements from our own experiments on a rope</div>
205	</section>
206	
207	<!-- ====================================================================== 02 -->
208	<section class="slide" data-title="Lecture Goals: From Video to a Simulator">
209	  <p class="lead">A digital twin is a physics model that a robot can push, grasp and learn on</p>
210	  <div class="content">
211	    <div class="row" style="margin-top:10px">
212	      <div class="blk t-blue" style="width:250px;height:124px"><span class="nm">Observation</span><span class="en">3 RGB-D cameras</span></div>
213	      <div class="arw" style="width:40px;margin-right:18px"></div>
214	      <div class="blk t-orange" style="width:250px;height:124px"><span class="nm">Particles</span><span class="en">+ spring graph</span></div>
215	      <div class="arw" style="width:40px;margin-right:18px"></div>
216	      <div class="blk t-green" style="width:250px;height:124px"><span class="nm">Physics</span><span class="en">springs · contact · ground</span></div>
217	      <div class="arw" style="width:40px;margin-right:18px"></div>
218	      <div class="blk t-purple" style="width:250px;height:124px"><span class="nm">Robot learning</span><span class="en">teleop · RL · evaluation</span></div>
219	    </div>
220	    <div class="abs" style="left:0;right:0;top:186px">
221	      <p class="sub-h">Three questions for this lecture</p>
222	      <p class="def"><b>Q1 — Simulation model</b> : how does a cloud of particles behave like a rope? (springs, dashpots, drag, integrator)</p>
223	      <p class="def"><b>Q2 — Contact model</b> : how do the ground, the rope itself and a robot gripper push back — and how is friction written?</p>
224	      <p class="def"><b>Q3 — Cost and consequences</b> : what makes it slow, and what does contact do to repeatability and learning?</p>
225	    </div>
226	    <div class="abs" style="left:0;right:0;bottom:12px">
227	      <p class="def" style="margin:0"><b>Sources</b> : PhysTwin (arXiv 2503.17973) · real2sim-eval (arXiv 2511.04665) · <span class="red">our projects 13–21 on the released rope twin</span></p>
228	    </div>
229	  </div>
230	</section>
231	
232	<!-- ====================================================================== 03 -->
233	<section class="slide" data-title="Two Simulators, One Spring Network">
234	  <p class="lead">Same particles and springs; they differ in how the hand or gripper touches the object</p>
235	  <div class="content">
236	    <table class="t sm">
237	      <tr><th style="width:290px">&nbsp;</th><th style="width:430px">PhysTwin</th><th>real2sim-eval</th></tr>
238	      <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>fit physics to a video (system ID)</td><td>evaluate robot policies</td></tr>
239	      <tr><td>Hand / gripper</td><td>control points <b>welded by springs</b></td><td>finger <b>triangle mesh, frictional contact</b></td></tr>
240	      <tr><td>Can the object slip or drop?</td><td>no — the springs never release</td><td>yes — held only by friction</td></tr>
241	      <tr><td>Gradients</td><td>differentiable (Warp tape)</td><td>forward only, overwritten in place</td></tr>
242	      <tr><td>Self-collision distance</td><td>0.02 m (off for real data)</td><td>0.005 m (on)</td></tr>
243	      <tr><td>Time step</td><td colspan="2">\(\Delta t=5\times10^{-5}\) s, \(\ 667\) substeps per 30 fps frame (both)</td></tr>
244	      <tr><td>Rope twin (rope_0001)</td><td colspan="2">2,002 particles · 17,048 object springs · 514 mm long · 31.5 mm across</td></tr>
245	    </table>
246	    <div class="abs" style="left:0;right:0;bottom:12px">
247	      <p class="def" style="margin:0"><b>real2sim-eval §III-C</b> : “we avoid the common but unrealistic practice of fixing object nodes to the gripper.” <span class="red">Everything about contact in this lecture follows from that choice.</span></p>
248	    </div>
249	  </div>
250	</section>
251	
252	<!-- ====================================================================== 04 -->
253	<section class="slide" data-title="State and the Spring Graph">
254	  <p class="lead">Every particle has \(\mathbf x,\ \mathbf v,\ m=1\); springs join neighbours found at frame 0</p>
255	  <div class="content">
